* Language logic::
@end menu

@node Language logic
@subsection Language logic

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{@strong{Header}}@strong{Header}


A file in @samp{Franc C} always starts with: ``Bonjour,'' followed by an empty
line.

Politeness is very important, and we wanted @samp{Franc C} to express a
proper and classic language, not a crappy street language. We promise
you will learn the ``bien parlé'' by using @samp{Franc C}

@item
@anchor{@strong{Function Declaration}}@strong{Function Declaration}


In order to declare a function, you must specify:

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{The name of the function}The name of the function


@example
J'aimerais définir le bloc répondant au nom de <function_name>,
@end example

where is the name of the function

@item
@anchor{The return type of the function}The return type of the function


@example
de type de retour entier naturel,
@end example

for the function to return an integer.

@example
de type de retour booléen,
@end example

for the function to return a boolean.

@example
de type de retour nul,
@end example

if the function does not return anything. (void function)

@item
@anchor{The parameters of the function}The parameters of the function


A parameter, in Franc C, is defined the same way as other programming
languages : Those are variables that must be passed when the function is
invoked for it to use them.

@example
necéssitant comme entrée :
    - <variable_name1>, de type <type>
    - <variable_name2>, de type <type>
    ...
;
@end example

You can include as many parameter as you want as long as they @strong{do not
share} the same name.

@itemize
@item
@samp{<variable_name1>} and @samp{<variable_name2>} are the name of the
variables which are both different.
@item
@samp{<type>} is either @samp{booléen} if the parameter is a @samp{boolean} or
@samp{entier naturel} if it is an @samp{int}.
@end itemize

Parameters are obviously optional. If the function does not take any
parameters, you must specify :

@example
nécessitant comme entrée : ;
@end example

@item
@anchor{Variables}Variables


Variables created and used inside a function, in Franc C, are defined in
the function definition. Since Franc C is a very verbose language and
uses a lot of words, creating variables at the top of the function makes
it a very good way to clearly see them.

Such variables are being declared the following way :

@example
contenant les variables :
    - <variable_name1>, de type <type>, valant <value>
    - <variable_name2>, de type <type>, valant <value>
;
@end example

Just like @strong{parameters}, @strong{variables} must not share the same name. A
@strong{parameter} and a @strong{variable} can not share the same name either.

@itemize
@item
@samp{<variable_name1>} and @samp{<variable_name2>} are the name of the
variables which are both different.
@item
@samp{<type>} is either @samp{booléen} if the variable is a @samp{boolean} or
@samp{entier naturel} if it is an @samp{int}.
@item
@samp{<value>} is either a number or a boolean expression @samp{vrai} @samp{vraie}
@samp{faux} or @samp{fausse}. It can also be a character value like @samp{\n} (which
will be 10).
@end itemize

This @strong{forces} every single created variable to have a default value.

If the function does not use any variable, you must specify :

@example
contenant les variables : ;
@end example
@end enumerate

@item
@anchor{@strong{Function Body}}@strong{Function Body}


A function declaration ends with

@example
représenté par le code <logical_connector>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<logical_connector>} is either @samp{ci-après}, @samp{suivant}, or
@samp{ci-dessous}.
@end itemize

What must follow this declaration is the @strong{body of the function}, which
is what @strong{will be executed} when the function is @strong{invoked}.

In Franc C, what can be executed inside a function is being refered as
an @samp{instruction}.

Those are the available instructions that you can execute inside a
function :

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Assignement}Assignement


You can, at any time, change the value of a variable the following way :

@example
J'aimerais que <variable_name> prenne la valeur <computables>.
@end example

or

@example
<variable_name> prends la valeur <computables>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<variable_name>} represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function.
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{If}If


Execute a block of code only if a specific condition was met. It can be
defined the following way :

@example
Si <computables>, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions>
Merci.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@item
@samp{<more_instructions>} represents all the instructions that will be
executed @strong{ONLY} if the @strong{condition} was @strong{met}.
@item
@samp{Merci.} represents the end of the condition.
@end itemize

You can also execute a block of code if the condition wasn't met the
following way :

@example
Si <computables>, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions>
; sinon, exécute le texte :
    <more_instructions_2>
Merci.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@item
@samp{<more_instructions>} represents all the instructions that will be
executed @strong{ONLY} if the @strong{condition} was @strong{met}.
@item
@samp{<more_instructions_2>} represents all the instructions that will be
executed @strong{ONLY} if the @strong{condition} was @strong{not met}.
@item
@samp{Merci.} represents the end of the condition.
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{Invoke}Invoke


Invoke another function inside a function, you can also store its result
in a well-defined function variable.

@example
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<function_name>} is the name of an @strong{existing defined function}.
@end itemize

If the function that is being invoked takes parameters, they @strong{must} be
specified the following way :

@example
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, avec les paramètres <computables>, <computables>, <computables>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<function_name>} is the name of an @strong{existing defined function}.
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@end itemize

You @strong{must} not give to a function more parameters than it takes.

In order to store the result of a function, while still giving
parameters :

@example
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, et j'assigne la valeur de retour à la variable <variable_name>, avec les paramètres <computables>, <computables>, <computables>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<function_name>} is the name of an @strong{existing defined function}.
@item
@samp{<variable_name>} represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function.
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@end itemize

However, if you do not want to give any parameter :

@example
J'invoque le bloc <function_name>, et j'assigne la valeur de retour à la variable <variable_name>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<function_name>} is the name of an @strong{existing defined function}.
@item
@samp{<variable_name>} represents the name of an existing well-defined
variable of the function
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{Loop}Loop


The only type of loop that is currently implemented in @samp{Franc C} are the
@samp{while} loops.

A loop allows to execute a block of code until a condition cannot be met
anymore.

@example
Tant que <computables> exécute le code ci-après :
    <more_instructions>
Merci.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@item
@samp{<more_instructions>} represents all the instructions that will be
executed @strong{ONLY} if the @strong{condition} was @strong{met}.
@item
@samp{Merci.} represents the end of the loop.
@end itemize

@item
@anchor{Return}Return


During the execution of the function, you can return at any time a
@strong{value}. Returning a value will cause the function execution to @strong{stop}.

The only condition to return a value is that the function must not be of
type ``nul''.

@example
Enfin, renvoie <computables>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@end itemize

If the function does not return anything (@samp{nul} function), you can still
stop the execution at any time.

@example
Enfin, sors du bloc.
@end example

This works only if the function is a @samp{nul} function.

@item
@anchor{Show statements}Show statements


This instruction is used to display either a text, or the result of a
computation in the screen.

To display a computation :

@example
Affiche <computables>.
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<computables>} represents an expression composed of values with
operators. (*Check the @samp{<condition>} defintion on the BNF)
@end itemize

This will display @strong{the character} corresponding to the result on the
screen. (E.g): @samp{Affiche 10.} will display @samp{\n}.

However, if you want to display a text :

@example
Affiche « Meow ».
@end example

This will display the text @samp{Meow} on the screen.
@end enumerate

@item
@anchor{@strong{End of Function}}@strong{End of Function}


You must @strong{always} end your @strong{Function Body} with a @samp{Merci.}.

@item
@anchor{@strong{Special case of 'Main' Function}}@strong{Special case of 'Main' Function}


The @strong{main function} is where the program starts. Therefore, its
defintion must be clearly different from the @strong{other functions}.

Such functions are being defined the following way :

@example
Bonjour,

En sachant que les variables principales sont :
    <variables>
; pourrais-tu s'il te plaît commencer la lecture ici ?
    <instructions>
Merci d'avance,
Cordialement,
<family_name>
<name>
@end example

@itemize
@item
@samp{<variables>} is an optional field, and represents the variables of
the function. They are declared the same way as a normal function.
@item
@samp{<instructions>} represents all the instructions that will be
executed.
@item
@samp{Merci d'avance, Cordialement, <family_name> <name>.} represents the
end of the main function
@item
@samp{<family_name>} represents the developer's last name, and must always
end with a newline character ('\n').
@item
@samp{<name>} represents the developer's first name, and must also end with
a newline character ('\n').
@end itemize

A @strong{main function} does not take any parameter. Therefore, no parameter
definition must be done. The value being @strong{returned} in the @strong{main
function} represent the exit status of the program. If no value was
being @strong{returned}, the program exists by default with a status code of
@samp{0}.
@end enumerate

@node Bytecode reference
@chapter Bytecode reference

The bytecode starts with a magic number, followed by an address
describing the placement of the PC at the start of the program, followed
by the program itself.

