@node Instructions
@section Instructions

Since the virtual machine is stack-based, every instruction representing
an operation will pop it's operands, and push the result to the stack.

@multitable {aaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem mnemonic
@tab description
@tab bnf object
@item not
@tab Not, invert the bits of the operand
@tab not
@item lnot
@tab Logical not, invert the boolean interpretation of the operand
@tab lnot
@item neg
@tab Negate, switch the sign of the operand
@tab neg
@item and
@tab And, performs a binary AND operation between two operands
@tab and
@item or
@tab Or, performs a binary OR operation between two operands
@tab or
@item xor
@tab Exclusive or, performs a binary XOR operation between two operands
@tab xor
@item shl
@tab Left bitshift, shifts the bits of the operand to the left, appending zeroes
@tab shl
@item shr
@tab Right bitshift, shifts the bits of the operand to the right, appending zeroes
@tab shr
@item land
@tab Logical and, boolean interpretation of the logical AND on two operands
@tab land
@item lor
@tab Logical or, boolean interpretation of the logical OR on two operands
@tab lor
@item add
@tab Addition, adds the value of two operands
@tab add
@item sub
@tab Substraction, subtract the value of two operands
@tab sub
@item mult
@tab Multiplication, multiplies the value of two operands
@tab mult
@item div
@tab Division, return the dividend of the euclidian division of operand 1 by operand 2
@tab div
@item mod
@tab Modulo, return the rest of the euclidian division of operand 1 by operand 2
@tab mod
@item gt
@tab Greater than, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab gt
@item ge
@tab Greater than or equals to, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab ge
@item lt
@tab Less than, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab lt
@item le
@tab Less than or equals to, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab le
@item eq
@tab Equals, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab eq
@item neq
@tab Different from, compares the two operands and return a result for boolean interpretation
@tab diff
@item updz
@tab Update zero flag, pops the value from the stack and set it as the new zero flag
@tab updz
@item push
@tab Push value, push a 64 bits value to the stack
@tab push@math{_value}
@item push
@tab Push relative address, push a 64 bits relative address to the stack, corresponding generally to a label
@tab push@math{_label}
@item push
@tab Push from stack pointer relative address, push to the stack the 8 bytes found at the stack pointer + the address given as operand
@tab push@math{_stack}
@item pop
@tab Pop empty, pop 8 bytes from the stack
@tab pop
@item pop
@tab Pop to stack pointer relative address, pop 8 bytes from the stack, and write them to the stack pointer + the address given as operand
@tab pop@math{_stack}
@item call
@tab Call, op 8 bytes from the stack, register the new stack pointer, push the PC and stack pointer to the callstack, and jump to the value of the 8 bytes popped from the stack
@tab call
@item ret
@tab Return, reset the stack to the stack pointer, pop the callstack and jump to the address from the callstack pop
@tab ret
@item jmp
@tab Jump, pop an address from the stack, and set the PC to it
@tab jmp
@item zjmp
@tab Zero jump, pop an address from the stack, and if the zero flag is set, set the PC to it
@tab zjmp
@item aff
@tab Show, pop 8 bytes from the stack, apply a modulo 256 to the result, and write a single ascii character corresponding to the result
@tab aff
@item affs
@tab Show string, the first operand is a 64 bits integer corresponding to the number of caracters, that we will call n. The next n bytes are the ascii values of the characters that will be wrote
@tab affs
@end multitable

@node Virtual Machine reference
@chapter Virtual Machine reference

@menu
* Introduction: Introduction (1). 
* Stack::
