@end menu

@node Introduction (1)
@section Introduction

When defining an assembly language for the Franc C binary, we focused on
the ease of development of the compiler. We just had one requirement
that allowed an easy development of the virtual machine: our assembly
shall be
@uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stack-oriented_programming, stack-based},
drawing inspiration from the
@uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_virtual_machine, Java Virtual
Machine (JVM)}.

@menu
* Stack-Based vs Register-Based::
@end menu

@node Stack-Based vs Register-Based
@subsection Stack-Based vs Register-Based

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Aspect
@tab Stack-Based (fcvm)
@tab Register-Based (e.g., Lua VM)
@item @strong{Storage}
@tab Operand stack (LIFO)
@tab Virtual registers
@item @strong{Instructions}
@tab Compact, implicit operands, prone to errors
@tab Explicit operand addressing
@item @strong{Complexity}
@tab Simple to implement
@tab More complex logic
@item @strong{Bytecode Size}
@tab Bigger overall (smaller instructions but way more quantiry)
@tab Smaller overall
@end multitable

We chose a stack-based approach for the following reasons:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Simpler bytecode generation}: The compiler does not have to deal
with registers, knowing it does not have the capacity to remember
data while processing multiple operations.
@item
@strong{Monotyped language}: The fact that the Franc C is an integer only
language allows for fixed-size parameter encoding. One of the main
advantages of the register-based approach is to allow for type
encoding.
@end enumerate

@node Stack
@section Stack

The stack shoud be implemented as a contiguous array of bytes with the
following characteristics:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Data type}: Array of bytes
@item
@strong{Element size}: 8 bytes per element (64-bit signed integers)
@item
@strong{Alignment}: All operations push/pop exactly 8 bytes
@item
@strong{Stack size}: Always a multiple of 8 bytes
@end itemize

@menu
* Initialization::
* Basic Operations::
@end menu

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Stack Management}Stack Management


To perform function calls, the VM must maintains the stack pointer. It
points to the start of the current stack local context. it is modified
by the instructions call and ret.
@end enumerate

@node Initialization
@subsection Initialization

By default, the stack should be initialized with 8 bytes representing
the return value of the program. The default value is
implementation-defined.

@node Basic Operations
@subsection Basic Operations

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Operation
@tab Description
@tab Stack Effect
@item @strong{PUSH}
@tab Push 8 bytes onto the stack
@tab SP unchanged, Counter += 1
@item @strong{POP}
@tab Remove 8 bytes from the stack
@tab SP unchanged, Counter -= 1
@end multitable

@node CallStack
@section CallStack

The @strong{callstack} manages function calls and returns, enabling the VM
to: - Navigate between functions - Restore execution context after
function returns - Maintain function-local state - Detect program
termination

@menu
* Memory Layout::
* Function call mechanism::
@end menu

@node Memory Layout
@subsection Memory Layout

The callstack can be implemented as a contiguous array of frame
records:@*
By default, the callstack can be initialized as an @strong{empty array}.

Example implementation : - @strong{Data type}: Array of tuples @samp{[(PC, SP)]} -
@strong{PC} (Program Counter): Return address (next instruction after CALL) -
@strong{SP} (Stack Pointer): Stack state at call time

@multitable {aaaaa} {aa} {aa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Frame
@tab PC
@tab SP
@tab Description
@item 0
@tab 0
@tab 0
@tab Entry point
@item 1
@tab 12
@tab 16
@tab First function call
@item 2
@tab 57
@tab 24
@tab Current function locale start
@end multitable

@node Function call mechanism
@subsection Function call mechanism

When executing @samp{call <address>}:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Save current context}: Push @samp{(PC + 1, SP)} onto the call stack
@itemize
@item
@samp{PC + 1}: Address of the instruction following CALL
@item
@samp{SP}: Current stack pointer (preserves caller's stack)
@end itemize
@item
@strong{Jump to function}: Set @samp{PC = <address>}
@end enumerate

When executing @samp{Ret}:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Pop frame}: Retrieve @samp{(saved_PC, saved_SP)} from call stack
@item
@strong{Restore context}:
@itemize
@item
Set @samp{PC = saved_PC} (return to caller)
@item
Set @samp{SP = saved_SP} (restore stack state)
@end itemize
@item
@strong{Check termination}: If call stack is empty after pop, @strong{terminate
program}
@end enumerate

@node Zero flag
@section Zero flag

The @strong{Zero Flag} is a single-bit register used for conditional branching
and control flow decisions.@*
It stores the result of comparison and test operations,@*
enabling the implementation of @samp{if} and @samp{while}.

@itemize
@item
@strong{Data type}: @samp{unsigned char}
@item
@strong{Default value}: @samp{1}
@end itemize

The Zero Flag follows this logic:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Condition
@tab Zero Flag Value
@item @strong{!= 0}
@tab @samp{1} (true)
@item @strong{== 0}
@tab @samp{0} (false)
@end multitable

@node End cases
@section End cases

The program terminates normally when: - A @samp{Ret} instruction is executed
from the main function - The call stack becomes empty after the return

